   249	
   250	## 4. Cost accounting with caching
   251	
   252	The paper compares **tokens**; whoever pays the bill compares **money**. An append-only
   253	transcript caches almost entirely; a mutating state block invalidates the prefix.
   254	
   255	Measured on Claude Haiku 4.5 via Microsoft Foundry (T=50, 3 seeds, caching on,
   256	short procedure):
   257	
   258	| Runtime | Score | Raw tokens | Effective cost | Cache saving |
   259	|---|---|---|---|---|
   260	| ReAct | 1.00 | 826k | **152k** | **82 %** |
   261	| Stateful | 1.00 | 873k | 873k | 0 % |
   262	| Memory | 0.87 | 313k | 313k | 0 % |
   263	| SKILL.state | 1.00 | **109k** | 109k | 0 % |
   264	
   265	**SKILL.state's advantage over ReAct goes from 7.54x in raw input tokens to 1.39x in
   266	effective input**, and the ordering inverts: by tokens SKILL.state < Memory < ReAct <
   267	Stateful; by effective input SKILL.state < **ReAct** < Memory < Stateful.
   268	
   269	Two bounds on that number, both material:
   270	
   271	- **These are input tokens, not a full invoice.** Output is not priced in. Mean output
   272	  per episode is 5,740 tokens for ReAct and 9,674 for SKILL.state — neither zero nor
   273	  equal, and they move the comparison in SKILL.state's disfavour.
   274	- **It depends on the length of the static prefix.** With the long procedure (~4,900
   275	  tokens, above the cacheable minimum), the arms that compress start caching too:
   276	  SKILL.state saves 79 %, Memory 48 %, Stateful 22 %, and SKILL.state's advantage is
   277	  2.51x rather than 1.39x. The conflict below is therefore **conditional on a short
   278	  static prefix**, not a property of compression as such.
   279	
   280	Three consequences:
   281	
   282	1. **With a short static prefix, compressing and caching are in conflict.** Every
   283	   method that compresses rewrites the prefix, and rewriting the prefix costs the
   284	   cache. With a long static prefix the conflict softens: the fixed block caches even
   285	   for the compressing arms.
   286	2. **ReAct and Stateful send nearly the same content and are billed 5.7x apart.**
   287	   ReAct saves 82 %, Stateful 0 %. This contrast does **not** isolate prompt order: the
   288	   two runners also differ in whether the history is marked as a cacheable prefix (only
   289	   ReAct marks it), and the Stateful figure was measured with the defective parser of §6
   290	   (item 7), whose state block was empty and therefore constant. Order, cache marking
   291	   and a mutating block are confounded here. **Isolated, the effect of order is 5.2x.**
   292	   Stateful on Haiku at T=50, 3 seeds × 2 runs per arm, both arms marking the history
   293	   as a cacheable prefix and sending the same content (~880k raw input tokens per
   294	   episode); only the position of the state block changes. State first: 74 % of the
   295	   input is cache *writes* and 22 % reads, an effective 869k per episode, a 1 % saving.
   296	   History first: 91 % reads and 4 % writes, an effective 168k, an 81 % saving. Scores do
   297	   not differ (0.997 and 1.000). One of the six state-first runs never applied a patch —
   298	   it wrote shelf numbers as unquoted keys, which is not JSON — so its state block stayed
   299	   constant and cached like the other arm (160k effective); excluding it, the ratio is
   300	   6.0x. We report 5.2x because it is what the arm as specified cost, and 6.0x as the
   301	   effect among runs whose state actually changed. Their Appendix A.3 template puts the state block first.
   302	3. **It suggests one reading of an anomaly in their table, which we cannot verify.**
   303	   Their totals column sits 2.7x to 3.7x below horizon × mean prompt across all twenty
   304	   cells. Caching is one hypothesis — totals billed, mean prompt raw — but their table
   305	   labels the totals as tokens consumed and gives no cache breakdown; the discrepancy is
   306	   unexplained until the authors say otherwise.
   307	
   308	### The magnitude does not transfer across providers
   309	
   310	Measured on Vertex with `gemini-3-flash-preview`, **within a single grid** (original
   311	environment, output cap 600, reasoning budget 0, n=5 episodes per cell) so that the
   312	comparison is not mixing conditions. The figure is cache-read tokens over total input
   313	tokens:
   314	
   315	| Runtime | T=25 | T=50 | T=100 | T=200 |
   316	|---|---|---|---|---|
   317	| ReAct | 1.1 % | 4.6 % | 6.8 % | **7.0 %** |
   318	| Stateful | 0 % | 2.6 % | 4.3 % | 4.3 % |
   319	| Memory, SKILL.state | 0 % | 0 % | 0 % | 0 % |
   320	
   321	Implicit caching therefore **exists on Vertex and favours exactly the arms whose prefix
   322	is append-only**, growing with the horizon — the same qualitative pattern as Anthropic,
   323	an order of magnitude smaller: **7 % against 82 %**. Other grids in other conditions give
   324	between 1 % and 16 % for ReAct; the spread across conditions is not characterised here.
   325	
   326	Paired within that grid, the cost ratio barely moves:
   327	
   328	| | Raw input | Effective input |
   329	|---|---|---|
   330	| T=50, ReAct / SKILL.state | 7.90x | **7.57x** |
   331	| T=200, ReAct / SKILL.state | 29.16x | **27.32x** |
   332	
   333	A direct probe adds a warning. The archived run (`results/probe_cache_vertex_*.json`)
   334	repeats an identical 13,346-token request twice and issues six calls whose prefix grows
   335	by ~3,300 tokens each: **none of the eight reads a single cached token**, while the grids
   336	of that same growing pattern registered 7 %. Implicit caching is **opportunistic and not
   337	reproducible on demand**. We report the grid figures, which are the ones the experiment
   338	actually incurred. (An earlier probe run, whose output was not archived, read cached
   339	tokens on the repeated request; we do not cite its figures.)
   340	
   341	> An earlier version of this section stated that Vertex has no implicit cache. It was
   342	> wrong: it generalised from the identical-request probe, and the project's own grids
   343	> contradicted it — 503,600 cached tokens in a single ReAct episode at T=200. A clean
   344	> measurement of the wrong thing.
   345	
   346	Explicit caching works (13,343 of 13,346 tokens discounted in the archived run) but does
   347	not fit ReAct's pattern: its prefix grows every step, so an object
   348	cached at step *k* covers a shrinking fraction of the prompt at step *k+n*. We did not
   349	measure whether repeatedly rebuilding it pays off; the claim here is only that the
   350	naive use — one fixed cached block — does not apply.
   351	
   352	Resulting statement: **with a short static prefix, prefix caching helps only the
   353	append-only arms, in both providers, and by an order of magnitude more on Anthropic;
   354	with a long enough immutable prefix it helps the compressing arms too (SKILL.state
   355	saves 79 % and Memory 48 %, above). The shrinking of SKILL.state's advantage measured on
   356	Anthropic does not reproduce on Vertex, where 7.9x in tokens remains 7.6x in effective
   357	input.**
   358	
   359	---
   360	
   361	## 5. Two probes on their declared limitations
   362	
   363	The paper declares two limitations without measuring them. We measured them.
   364	
   365	### L1: explicit state only protects what was anticipated
   366	
   367	A fact arrives at step `t` and changes the correct action at `t+40`; the measure is
   368	whether the agent gets that one step right. All three models, T=50, seeds 0–2 × 8
   369	repetitions per cell, output cap 8192 (Gemini with reasoning budget 0), Claude via
   370	Microsoft Foundry and Gemini via Vertex, with a runner that applies no action when the
   371	runtime returns none (§6, item 11). SKILL.state under four schema conditions:
   372	
   373	| Condition | Haiku 4.5 | Sonnet 5 | Gemini 3 Flash |
   374	|---|---|---|---|
   375	| No field to store it | 0/24 [0–14 %] | 3/24 [4–31 %] | 0/24 [0–14 %] |
   376	| Free-form `notes` field | 10/24 [24–61 %] | 8/24 [18–53 %] | 0/24 [0–14 %] |
   377	| Schema field naming the fact | **24/24 [86–100 %]** | **18/24 [55–88 %]** | **16/24 [47–82 %]** |
   378	| Reminder attached to the observation | **24/24 [86–100 %]** | **20/24 [64–93 %]** | 8/24 [18–53 %] |
   379	
   380	ReAct has no schema, so the first three conditions send it the identical prompt; pooled,
   381	they are 72 replicates of the same cell. Its reminder condition is the only one that
   382	changes what it sees:
   383	
   384	| ReAct | Haiku 4.5 | Sonnet 5 | Gemini 3 Flash |
   385	|---|---|---|---|
   386	| No reminder (72 runs) | 10/72 = 14 % [8–24 %] | 20/72 = 28 % [19–39 %] | 0/72 = 0 % [0–5 %] |
   387	| Reminder on the observation | **18/24 = 75 % [55–88 %]** | **23/24 = 96 % [80–99 %]** | **17/24 = 71 % [51–85 %]** |
   388	
   389	Wilson 95 % intervals in brackets. Three things hold on all three models:
   390	
   391	- **Naming the field works; giving it somewhere to go is not enough.** The named field
   392	  takes SKILL.state from 0–13 % to 67–100 %. The free-form `notes` field helps on the two
   393	  Claude models (Haiku 42 %, Sonnet 33 %) and not at all on Gemini (0 %), and on every
   394	  model it stays far below the named field. An explicit-state runtime protects best
   395	  against what its designer already anticipated — limitation L1, with a number.
   396	- **The reminder works for the runtime that has nowhere to store anything.** ReAct with
   397	  the fact attached to the observation goes from 0–28 % to 71–96 %.
   398	- **The successes concentrate by scenario, not by trial.** On Gemini, SKILL.state with
   399	  the named field scores 0/8, 8/8, 8/8 across the three seeds and with the reminder 0/8,
   400	  8/8, 0/8; binomial intervals over 24 runs understate the uncertainty of generalising
   401	  to new scenarios, and we do not claim the difference between those two Gemini cells.
   402	
   403	> **What changed with the re-measurement.** The earlier L1 figures came from a runner
   404	> that applied the correct action whenever the runtime returned none. With the new traces
   405	> the size of that bias is visible: at cap 8192 Haiku and Gemini leave no step without an
   406	> action before the dependent step, and Sonnet leaves 57, in 39 of its 192 episodes. On
   407	> Gemini, the one model whose earlier cells are archived with the same design, nothing
   408	> moved: SKILL.state scores 0, 0, 16 and 8 of 24 in both versions, and ReAct with the
   409	> reminder 16 then and 17 now. The earlier Sonnet cells (3, 5, 18 and 20 of 24) cannot be
   410	> reproduced from the archived artefacts, which hold smaller cohorts, so they are
   411	> replaced rather than compared. And the free-form field, measured on Haiku for the first
   412	> time, reaches 42 %: the earlier reading that "the hatch finds no support in either
   413	> model" does not generalise.
   414	>
   415	> This probe also **withdrew** an earlier recommendation of the project itself: with one
   416	> run per seed, the free-form field had scored 60 % on Sonnet and become the practical
   417	> advice ("leave a hatch in your schema"). With repetitions it stays well below a named
   418	> field on every model.
   419	
   420	### L2: retroactive invalidation
   421	
   422	An announced fact stops being true later, and the agent has to act on the correction.
   423	On a correct trajectory the correction decides **exactly one step per episode**; once an
   424	agent misses it, its world diverges and further steps start to disagree with the truth,
   425	so counting them scores the cascade, not the correction. The measure is therefore per
   426	episode: does the agent get the decisive step right? All three models at T=50, seeds 4,
   427	10 and 6 × 8 repetitions, output cap 8192 (Gemini with reasoning budget 0), Claude via
   428	Microsoft Foundry and Gemini via Vertex:
   429	
   430	| Model | ReAct applies the correction | SKILL.state |
   431	|---|---|---|
   432	| Haiku 4.5 | 1/24 | **24/24** |
   433	| Sonnet 5 | 9/24 | **22/24** |
   434	| Gemini 3 Flash | 0/24 | **24/24** |
   435	
   436	**Explicit state applies the correction in 70 of 72 episodes (97 %, Wilson 95 % interval
   437	90–99 %); full history in 10 of 72 (14 %, 8–24 %).** Every episode had its decisive step,
   438	and no response was truncated at it — seven Sonnet/ReAct episodes truncated elsewhere,
   439	and none was excluded. The misses are not other errors: every failed decisive step is
   440	exactly the action of an agent that never heard the correction. The successes
   441	concentrate by scenario rather than by trial — Sonnet with ReAct scores 2/8, 0/8 and
   442	7/8 across the three seeds, and its two SKILL.state misses are both in seed 4 — so the
   443	binomial intervals understate the uncertainty of generalising to new scenarios.
   444	
   445	> **This replaces an earlier measurement that did not measure what it published.** The
   446	> previous version of this section reported "93 of 93" and "132 of 132" dependent steps
   447	> with explicit state. Its scorer took the dependent steps from the trajectory of a
   448	> *simulated* deaf agent, counted successes as dependent steps minus deaf actions without
   449	> deducting steps with no action or with another error, and its runner applied the
   450	> *correct* action whenever the runtime returned none. A runtime that never answers
   451	> scored 100 % (§6, item 11). The direction survives; the perfection does not.
   452	
   453	### Across the three models
   454	
   455	Gemini with ReAct never applies either correction — 0 of 72 in L1 without a reminder, 0
   456	of 24 in L2 — while scoring 0.913 on the long procedure; Haiku scores 0.974 at T=200 and
   457	applies the L2 correction once in 24. What holds on all three models is that **the
   458	long-procedure score does not predict this failure, and within each model the runtime
   459	decides it.** Both probes are measured with the same cap and design on the three models;
   460	their decoding still differs between providers (§3).
   461	
   462	---
   463	
   464	## 6. Instrument and artefacts
   465	
   466	This is the section that separates this from a blog post, and the one that cost us most.
   467	
   468	**Nine artefacts catalogued** in the first block, all with the same signature: an
   469	implicit contract between runtime and model. The runtime assumed something it never
   470	declared, and the result depended on whether the model guessed right. The first three
   471	cost entire grids; **two surfaced in the audit before publication, and both had produced
   472	a written result, with tables and intervals, ready to send**.
   473	
   474	This second block added six more, and they are worth enumerating because they transfer:
   475	
   476	1. **Greedy decoding does not guarantee reproducibility.** The protocol assumed that at
   477	   `temperature=0` seeds are environment instances and one run per cell suffices. The
   478	   same cell repeated five times gives between 0.830 and 0.960. **Three already-written
   479	   conclusions collapsed on repetition**: an environment effect of −5.8 points that
   480	   turned out to be +0.008, a "monotonic" noise curve that turned out flat, and a
   481	   noise estimate of eleven points that turned out to be a tail.
   482	2. **The distribution has a left tail because failures cascade.** Across five runs of one
   483	   cell the sample deviation is 0.052; **excluding the 0.830 outlier** it is 0.012, and
   484	   three runs of the same cell at T=100 give 0.006. We quote both, because the second
   485	   number is conditional on removing the very run that motivates the point. What forces
   486	   repetition is not the ordinary spread: it is the tails.
   487	3. **The runner discarded the model's responses.** It kept the score and the token
   488	   counts, so `correct=False` was an indistinguishable zero: you could not tell whether
   489	   the model lost track of state — what the experiment measures — or miscopied a JSON
   490	   field. With responses retained, adjudicating 45 failures across four traces of one
   491	   cell gives **32 wrong-shelf (71 %), 7 wrong action type (16 %), 5 field-copy errors
   492	   (11 %), 1 unparseable**.
   493	4. **A truncated episode counted as complete.** Aggregation counted files, not steps; an
   494	   episode of 163 steps out of 200 entered the mean. The bias was two thousandths, but
   495	   it was only found because completeness was checked as a matter of routine.
   496	5. **Traces that did not record their own conditions.** For most of this work the traces
   497	   stored no output cap, reasoning budget, provider or responding model ID. Two files
   498	   from the same cell could come from different caps and nothing would say so — which is
   499	   how a −5.8-point "environment effect" survived long enough to be written down. The
   500	   runners now write a conditions header and per-step token usage — but **only runs made
   501	   after that change carry it**. The historical Gemini traces — Table 1 except the
   502	   Stateful re-measurement, the noise table and the environment comparison — do not, so
   503	   their conditions are certified by the filename and the run log, not by the artefact
   504	   itself. Every trace behind the Haiku table, both Stateful re-measurements, the Memory
   505	   control and the prompt-order experiment does.
   506	6. **A probe that measured the wrong pattern.** The cache probe repeated an identical
   507	   request and concluded that Vertex has no implicit cache, while the project's own
   508	   grids recorded 503,600 cached tokens in one episode. The probe was correct; the
   509	   pattern it probed was not the one the experiment uses.
   510	
   511	A third block, found while measuring the second model, contains the most expensive
   512	artefact of the project:
   513	
   514	7. **One of the four arms never ran.** Stateful read its state patch with a non-greedy
   515	   regex that stopped at the first closing brace. Every nested patch — the normal case,
   516	   `{"shelf_contents": {"3": {...}}}` — was truncated, failed to parse, and was dropped
   517	   without a warning: **40 of 5,775 responses on Gemini produced an applied patch, 0 of
   518	   6,000 on Haiku**. What both
   519	   Stateful columns measured is full history plus a state block with no inventory — empty
   520	   on Haiku, and on Gemini holding only the occasional `last_event` (the 40 patches, in
   521	   11 episodes). Its unit test
   522	   used a flat value and passed. It surfaced only because Stateful beat SKILL.state on
   523	   Haiku and we went to the traces to find out why. Re-measured with the parser fixed,
   524	   no change was detected (§3). The first fix was itself incomplete: on Haiku it still
   525	   dropped every patch written in Markdown — 731 of 3,000 at T=200 — which the third
   526	   adversarial review found, not us; a third parser version closed it. A defect that
   527	   changes nothing measurable is still a defect in what the column claims to measure.
   528	8. **A cap one model respects and the other does not.** The summariser's own cap was
   529	   never reached on Gemini and was exceeded in half the summaries on Haiku (§3). A cap
   530	   is not a neutral constant across models; it has to be checked per model, in the
   531	   traces.
   532	9. **Resuming re-paid finished work.** The completeness check counted the conditions
   533	   header as a step, so no episode ever matched its horizon and every relaunch re-ran
   534	   episodes already measured. It was caught on the first relaunch of R2 because a cell
   535	   finished minutes earlier started again.
   536	10. **The traces did not record cache writes.** The client received them; the trace row
   537	    did not store them. In the first run of the prompt-order experiment the arm that
   538	    rewrites its cache every step — the most expensive case — showed ~540 prompt tokens
   539	    per step and looked the cheapest. Those twelve episodes were discarded and re-run.
   540	    The §4 table does not depend on it: it was computed by the grid runner, which counted
   541	    writes.
   542	
   543	11. **A probe that could not fail.** The first L2 scorer counted dependent steps on a
   544	    simulated trajectory and subtracted only deaf actions, and its runner repaired the
   545	    world whenever the runtime returned no action; a runtime that never answers scored
   546	    100 %. It was the section of this draft we had checked least, because its result was
   547	    the cleanest. The fifth adversarial review found it. Re-measured with the decisive
   548	    step read from the real trajectory, explicit state goes from "132 of 132" to 70 of
   549	    72 episodes. L1 had the same world-repairing runner; re-measured, its cells barely
   550	    moved, but only the new traces could show that.
   551	
   552	Items 5 and 6 were found by an **independent adversarial review of this draft against
   553	the raw traces**, not by us. That review also corrected the failure classification
   554	above, the maintenance-event range in §2, the compression factor in §3 and the sample
   555	size in §3. We recommend the procedure, not the anecdote: have something recompute every
   556	published figure from the artefacts, without access to the prose that justifies it.
   557	
   558	Why traces matter, verbatim from one episode: at step 12 the model stores the SKU of a
   559	different pallet; at step 16 it asserts *"Shelf 0 (SKU-F, stored in step 12)"* when it
   560	had itself stored SKU-B there. **The early error contaminates later decisions** — and
   561	that, not sampling noise, is what produces the tail.
   562	
   563	**The transferable claim**: the paper treats the runtime as neutral infrastructure — a
   564	merge operator, a patch format, a schema, a prompt order. In this project, individual
   565	runtime decisions moved results by amounts comparable to or larger than the difference
   566	between the methods being compared: the schema field decides 0 % versus 67 % on the
   567	dependent step (§5), and a parser that silently dropped state patches decided what one
   568	of four arms measured until it was fixed (§6, item 7). We have not measured every such
   569	decision, so we make no claim about a general range.
   570	
   571	**Repository artefacts**: environment with fidelity flags, per-step traces, completeness
   572	verifier, a density meter that does not call the API, truncation counters, per-episode
   573	checkpointing and end-to-end cache accounting.
   574	
   575	---
   576	
   577	## 7. Limitations
   578	
   579	- **`gemini-3-flash-preview` may not be their `Gemini-3-Flash`.** Declared as a risk
   580	  from the outset. Vertex returns the responding model ID on every call and it is
   581	  recorded per step, but not in the traces measured before that change. The Anthropic
   582	  client did not copy it until the last Stateful re-measurement: from then on — its 30
   583	  episodes and the prompt-order experiment — every step records
   584	  `claude-haiku-4-5-20251001`; the earlier Haiku traces record only the requested alias
   585	  in their header.
   586	- **A reimplemented environment is not their environment.** We matched what their
   587	  appendix allows us to compare, and the remaining differences are in §2. The shelf
   588	  choice in `Store` still awaits an answer from the authors.
   589	- **Our Memory is not comparable to theirs.** Our summariser compresses fourteen times
   590	  more (5,770-token mean prompt against their 84,364 at T=200).
   591	  What we measured is our summarisation policy, not "summarisation" as a category.
   592	- **We have not proven the environment gaps have no effect.** The measured difference is
   593	  +0.009, with an interval of roughly −0.040 to +0.057 (§2): no change was detected, and
   594	  effects of several points in either direction remain compatible. The same caveat
   595	  applies to the three lowest noise levels in §3.
   596	- **Cell sizes are not uniform.** Nineteen cells carry 15 runs; ReAct at T=200 carries
   597	  19, unbalanced across seeds (3/3/7/3/3). Five of its episodes exist only as
   598	  aggregates, without traces.
   599	- **Cost figures price input only**, and the caching results depend on the length of the
   600	  static prefix (§4).
   601	- **The probes share cap and design across models, not decoding.** Both were re-measured
   602	  on all three models with the corrected runner (§5); Gemini runs with temperature 0 and
   603	  reasoning budget 0, Claude without either setting.
   604	- **One environment.** The second (Software Repository) was retired because it could not
   605	  measure what it was built for, not because the arms scored alike — they did not
   606	  (Haiku: SKILL.state 0.974, ReAct 0.895; Sonnet: 0.876 and 1.000, 9 episodes each). Its
   607	  dependency always sat exactly two steps after the event that created it, so it had no
   608	  delayed relevance to measure; all four arms applied that rule (Haiku SKILL.state 14/15,
   609	  ReAct 12/12; Sonnet SKILL.state 12/13, ReAct all); and the score differences came from
   610	  routine steps, not from the rule (`docs/resultados-bloque1.md`, §4.ter).
   611	- **SKILL.state's ceiling depends on the model.** At 1.000 with zero variance on Gemini
   612	  the task did not discriminate at the top; on Haiku it does (0.958 at T=200), and the
   613	  failure is in writing the state (§3). Two models are not enough to say which models
   614	  make that error.
   615	- **Stateful has been re-measured only at T=50 and T=200**, with parser version 2 on
   616	  Gemini (which applied 3,748 of 3,750 of its patches) and version 3 on Haiku. The
   617	  intermediate horizons of Gemini's Table 1 keep the defective measurement, marked as
   618	  such.
   619	- **Haiku and Gemini do not share decoding settings** (§3): the cross-model differences
   620	  are between two configured systems, not two models under identical conditions.
   621	- **Haiku's Memory column mixes the summarisation policy with summary truncation** (§3);
   622	  the control arm has n=9 and bounds the effect at T=50 only.

