     1	# The Runtime Is Not Infrastructure: A Replication of SKILL.state
     2	
     3	**Draft.** Every figure comes from a measurement in this repository, with per-step
     4	traces and raw data on the `runs/table1-gemini-3-flash-preview-vertex` branch.
     5	
     6	---
     7	
     8	## 1. What the paper claims, and what we measured
     9	
    10	[SKILL.state](https://arxiv.org/abs/2608.26263) argues that an agent maintaining
    11	**explicit state** executes long procedures far better than one carrying the whole
    12	conversation, and that the gap widens with the horizon. Its Table 1 shows this with
    13	Gemini-3-Flash in a simulated warehouse: ReAct falls from 0.90 to 0.74 between 10 and
    14	200 steps while SKILL.state falls only from 1.00 to 0.94.
    15	
    16	We reimplemented their environment from the paper's description — SkillExecBench is not
    17	public — and measured three things:
    18	
    19	1. **Whether the ladder reproduces** under their own model and decoding settings (§3).
    20	2. **What each runtime actually costs** when you count money rather than tokens (§4).
    21	3. **What explicit state protects against**, through two probes aimed at the
    22	   limitations the paper itself declares but does not measure (§5).
    23	
    24	The short version: **their thesis holds and their magnitude does not; and once input is
    25	billed with caching, SKILL.state's cost advantage over full history shrinks from 7.5x to
    26	1.4x on Anthropic with a short static prefix** — it stays the cheapest arm, and on Vertex
    27	the advantage barely moves. Explicit state protects against losing track of
    28	the procedure; on a second model it does not protect against **writing the state
    29	wrong**, and that failure — not the one the paper studies — is what ends up limiting it.
    30	And the largest effects in the whole experiment come not from any of the compared
    31	methods, but from runtime implementation decisions the paper treats as neutral: one of
    32	them silently disabled one of our four arms in every measurement made before we fixed it
    33	(§6).
    34	
    35	---
    36	
    37	## 2. Environment fidelity, and the differences we declare
    38	
    39	The paper publishes the environment design (Appendix B.1), the generator pseudocode
    40	(Algorithm 2) and the prompts (Appendix A), so the comparison is not blind.
    41	
    42	**What matches**: 500 single-occupancy shelves, the action space
    43	(`Store`/`Ship`/`Move`/`Wait`), item destruction on `Ship`, the metric (valid actions
    44	executed / actionable events) and per-seed determinism.
    45	
    46	**What did not match, and is now closed behind flags** (`--apendice-b`,
    47	`--sin-telemetria`):
    48	
    49	| Difference | What we did |
    50	|---|---|
    51	| No maintenance events, so `Move` was never exercised | Implemented: 11–20 per 50-step episode |
    52	| Invalid actions were applied rather than rejected | Rejection with a local error observation, as in their B.1 |
    53	| A third of our steps were non-actionable telemetry | Removed: their Algorithm 2 has no non-actionable family |
    54	
    55	**Closing all three produces no detectable change.** ReAct at T=200, same output cap:
    56	0.905 ± 0.071 in the original environment (n=15) and 0.913 ± 0.072 in the corrected one
    57	(n=19). Difference +0.009, standard error 0.025 — an interval of roughly −0.040 to
    58	+0.057. This is a failure to detect an effect at this power, **not evidence that there
    59	is none**; anything below ~5 points would be invisible here.
    60	
    61	**Differences that remain, declared**:
    62	
    63	- **Our actions carry four fields** (`shelf`, `sku`, `units`, `lot`) against their
    64	  positional two. There is more to get right per action, which cuts against the
    65	  "our environment is easier" hypothesis. The failure adjudication (§6) quantifies it
    66	  at 11 %.
    67	- **Our observations are more verbose.** The match is by **context density**, not
    68	  literal content. At T=200, ReAct's mean prompt is 58,127 tokens against their 48,007
    69	  — **above theirs**.
    70	- **Which empty shelf their ground truth picks in `Store`** is unspecified. We take the
    71	  lowest-numbered free shelf. This is the one difference that requires asking the
    72	  authors.
    73	
